[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128983 :question/id #uuid "5110af12-1d0a-5f7e-8a3b-626cc437c53e" :question/math-academy-id "e-6097" :question/problem "Evaluate the repeated integral $\\displaystyle \\int_{2}^{4}  \\int_{0}^{2} \\dfrac{x^3}{y^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x  \\, \\textrm{d}y.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:    \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{4}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}{[\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}(\\frac{{2}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{{0}^{4}}{4})\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{4}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y & =4{[-\\frac{1}{y}]}_{2}^{4} \\\\  & =4(-\\frac{1}{4}-\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128984 :question/id #uuid "bd93891e-1f9f-51af-a5c2-4eb5ca1178d9" :question/math-academy-id "e-6098" :question/problem "Evaluate the double integral $\\displaystyle \\iint \\limits_{D} \\dfrac y{x^2} \\, \\textrm d A$, where \n$D=\\left\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\:2 \\leq x \\leq 4,\\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 3\\right\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express our double integral as a repeated integral:    \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}[{\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}[{\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{[-\\frac{y}{x}]}_{2}^{4}\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(-\\frac{y}{4}-\\left(-\\frac{y}{2}\\right))\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{y}{4}\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{y}{4}\\,\\text{d}y & ={[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{8}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{{3}^{2}}{8}-\\frac{{0}^{2}}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129002 :question/id #uuid "3e024f68-63e5-5080-ba74-c806f6af9036" :question/math-academy-id "e-6221" :question/problem "The point $P$ has cylindrical polar coordinates $\\left(4, \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}, -8 \\right).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P.$" :question/worked-solution "If $(r, \\theta, z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx = r\\cos\\theta, \\qquad\ny = r\\sin\\theta, \\qquad \nz = z.\n$$\n\nCalculating $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $y$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(2\\sqrt3, 2, -8).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129004 :question/id #uuid "7007e89d-fe92-5cc9-a0d1-49f34036176f" :question/math-academy-id "e-6226" :question/problem "The point $Q$ has spherical coordinates $\\left(2\\sqrt3, \\pi, \\dfrac{5\\pi}{6} \\right).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $Q.$" :question/worked-solution "If $(\\rho, \\theta, \\phi)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx = \\rho\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad\ny = \\rho\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad \nz = \\rho\\cos\\phi.\n$$\n\nCalculating $x,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\cdot (-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\cdot 0\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $z,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $Q$ are $(-\\sqrt3, 0, -3).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129005 :question/id #uuid "cadaa861-db49-58bc-97a5-42c3dda4fa75" :question/math-academy-id "e-6227" :question/problem "The point $P$ has Cartesian coordinates $(-\\sqrt2,0,\\sqrt2).$ What are the spherical polar coordinates of  $P?$" :question/worked-solution "If $(\\rho, \\theta, \\phi)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx = \\rho\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad \ny = \\rho\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad \nz = \\rho\\cos\\phi.\n$$\n\nFrom the above equations, we can express $\\rho$, $\\theta$, and $\\phi$ in terms of $x$, $y$, and $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\rho = \\sqrt{x^2+y^2+z^2}, \\qquad\n\\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{y}{x}, \\qquad\n\\cos\\phi = \\dfrac{z}{\\rho}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $\\rho\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho  & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-\\sqrt{2})^{2}+{0}^{2}+{(\\sqrt{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2+0+2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $(x,y) = (-\\sqrt2,0)$ lies on the negative $x$-axis, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, for $\\phi,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ϕ & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡\\left(\\frac{z}{\\rho }\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the spherical coordinates of $P$ are $\\left(2, \\pi,  \\dfrac{\\pi}4 \\right).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129015 :question/id #uuid "9de92581-00d5-5343-90be-3b5527bfab49" :question/math-academy-id "e-7283" :question/problem "Using the change of variables \n\n$$\nu =3x-2y, \\quad v=x+y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the curve $(3x-2y)^2 - y - x = 0$ and the line $x+y=4.$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} \\mathrm d x \\mathrm d y.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $\\mathbf T$ as follows: \n\n![](images/1e/1efa09fa657f5c016d251adfd8db31d473bf901b07efea0c0f40c4ef161cc8f3.svg)\n\nThis transformation maps some region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} \\mathrm d x \\mathrm d y = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\right|  \\ \\mathrm d u \\mathrm d v\n$$\n\nwhere $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu = 3x-2y, \\qquad v=x+y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function $\\mathbf T^{-1},$ that is\n\n![](images/e1/e1236dd6d81a7cb3217fa4b64989220dcfde67d04a5252eef02f2f3ce0b33c20.svg)\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $\\Delta,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}.$ \n\nSubstituting $3x-2y = u$ and $x+y = v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3x-2y)^{2}-y-x=0\\,⟹\\,{u}^{2}-v=0\\,⟹\\,v={u}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $x + y = v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+x=4\\,⟹\\,v=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}v &={u}^{2} \\\\ v &=4\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}u &=\\pm 2 \\\\ v &=4.\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm 2, 4).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\big\\{ (u,v) \\: : \\: -2 \\leq u \\leq 2, \\, u^2 \\leq v \\leq 4\\big\\},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/0a/0a3d0211dfbe18e5737b62e8b61cf9308af2735ef827d14bae7b5dbfffaa4faa.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}{:}$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T{:}$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} = \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial (u, v)}{\\partial (x, y)} \\right)^{-1} = (5)^{-1} = \\dfrac 15.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\neq 0$ everywhere inside $\\Delta.$  In other words, $\\mathbf T$ has no critical points inside $\\Delta.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{5}\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}[{\\int }_{{u}^{2}}^{4}\\,dv]du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}[v]_{{u}^{2}}^{4}\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}(4-{u}^{2})\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot 2{\\int }_{0}^{2}(4-{u}^{2})\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}{[4u-\\frac{{u}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}(8-\\frac{8}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}\\left(\\frac{16}{3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129016 :question/id #uuid "d3ae6c85-14bd-57ad-b8cc-1cebb53683b7" :question/math-academy-id "e-7346" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\pi/8} \\int_0^{\\sin2\\theta} r \\, \\textrm d r\\,\\textrm d \\theta.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we integrate with respect to $r,$ treating $\\theta$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{[{r}^{2}]}_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{sin}^{2}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the double-angle formula\n\n$$\n\\cos{2x} = 1-2\\sin^2 x.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2\\theta$ and rearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\sin^22\\theta = \\dfrac12\\left(1-\\cos4\\theta\\right).\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we can finish our integral by integrating with respect to $\\theta,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{sin}^{2}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{[\\theta -\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta ]}_{0}^{\\pi /8} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(\\frac{\\pi }{8}-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right))-(0-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{\\pi }{8}-\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{\\pi }{8}-\\frac{2}{8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{32}(\\pi -2)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129018 :question/id #uuid "f31dd085-70fa-5eed-adf9-89fb0e6654e1" :question/math-academy-id "e-7348" :question/problem "Integrate the function $f(x, y) = 15xy^2$ over the portion of the disk of radius $2$ centered at the origin, where $x \\geq 0,y \\geq 0.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r\\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\:  r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/05/051cfbbf3fd37f01ff7e4442fccdc5ec4ce506e3c452b68bfa2ed5ae6e5cf6b9.png)\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r, \\theta),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\left\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq r \\leq 2, \\:0\\leq \\theta \\leq  \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}15x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}15r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot (r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}15{r}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{2}5{r}^{4}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,{[{r}^{5}]}_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ({2}^{5}-0)\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =32{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =32{[{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta ]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =32({sin}^{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-{sin}^{3}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =32(1-0) \\\\  & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129034 :question/id #uuid "fdf51620-58e9-5886-93e5-02a814b154bb" :question/math-academy-id "e-8905" :question/problem "Consider the arc of the curve $y=e^x$ that lies between the lines $x=0$ and $x=2.$ What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS = \\int 2\\pi y\\,\\textrm d s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y = f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\textrm d s = \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS = 2\\pi \\int_{x_1}^{x_2} y \\sqrt{1+ \\left( \\dfrac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\right)^2} \\: \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from $x_1=0$ to $x_2={2}.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})={e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{x}\\sqrt{1+({e}^{x})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{x}\\sqrt{1+{e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129035 :question/id #uuid "ae72af2a-ec7e-569d-89a8-550c8eeabdbe" :question/math-academy-id "e-8909" :question/problem "Consider the arc of the curve $y=\\sqrt{x-2}$ that lies between the lines $x=2$ and $x=4.$ Calculate the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS = \\int 2\\pi y\\,\\textrm d s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y = f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\textrm d s = \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS = 2\\pi \\int_{x_1}^{x_2} y \\sqrt{1+ \\left( \\dfrac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\right)^2} \\: \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from $x_1=2$ to $x_2=4.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{x-2})=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x-2}}\\,⇒\\,{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{2}=\\frac{1}{4(x-2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{4}\\sqrt{x-2}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{4(x-2)}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{4}\\sqrt{x-2}\\sqrt{\\frac{4x-7}{4(x-2)}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{4}\\sqrt{x-2}\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{4x-7}}{2\\sqrt{x-2}}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{4}\\sqrt{4x-7}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the substitution \n\n$$\nu=4x-7 \\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad \\textrm du = 4\\,\\textrm dx,\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{4}\\sqrt{4x-7}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}{\\int }_{1}^{9}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}\\,|}_{1}^{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}(27-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{13\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129036 :question/id #uuid "fcfc9339-efd3-525f-a6f3-695124724e1c" :question/math-academy-id "e-9120" :question/problem "Consider the arc of the curve $x=y^3-y$ that lies in the second quadrant between its $y$-intercepts. What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis?\n\n![](images/1b/1bdbe21cbb517645dfe9362f3313c2d85118acfea1d839fe33ae9b9ac6c3ed50.png)" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS = \\int 2\\pi y\\,\\textrm d s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x = f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\textrm d s = \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d y}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS = 2\\pi \\int_{y_1}^{y_2} y \\sqrt{1+ \\left( \\dfrac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} \\right)^2} \\: \\textrm{d}y.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we find where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{3}-y & =0 \\\\ y({y}^{2}-1) & =0 \\\\ y & =0,\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=1.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{3}-y)=3{y}^{2}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\sqrt{1+{(3{y}^{2}-1)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\sqrt{2-6{y}^{2}+9{y}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129045 :question/id #uuid "418e4c16-f718-53ec-af1d-bc871c175c1d" :question/math-academy-id "e-9600" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the difference equation $a_n + 100a_{n-2} =0$." :question/worked-solution "Let\n\n$$\na_n=\\lambda^n\n$$\n\nThen, we have\n\n$$\na_{n-1} = \\lambda^{n-1}, \\qquad a_{n-2} = \\lambda^{n-2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our difference equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ^{n} + 100λ^{n - 2} &= 0 \\\\\nλ^{n - 2}(λ^{2} + 100) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ^{2} + 100 &= 0 \\\\\nλ^{2} &= -100 \\\\\nλ &= ± 10\\textrm{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1 = 10\\textrm i\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\lambda_2 = -10\\textrm i\n$$\n\nExpressing our solutions in polar form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ_{1} &= 10(\\cos \\left(\\frac{π}{2}\\right) + \\textrm{i}\\sin \\left(\\frac{π}{2}\\right)), \\\\\nλ_{2} &= 10(\\cos \\left(\\frac{π}{2}\\right) - \\textrm{i}\\sin \\left(\\frac{π}{2}\\right))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using De Moivre's theorem, we can write the general solution to the difference equation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= Cλ_{1}^{n} + Dλ_{2}^{n} \\\\\n&= C[10(\\cos \\left(\\frac{π}{2}\\right) + \\textrm{i}\\sin \\left(\\frac{π}{2}\\right))]^{n} + D[10(\\cos \\left(\\frac{π}{2}\\right) - \\textrm{i}\\sin \\left(\\frac{π}{2}\\right))]^{n} \\\\\n&= C[10^{n}(\\cos \\left(\\frac{nπ}{2}\\right) + \\textrm{i}\\sin \\left(\\frac{nπ}{2}\\right))] + D[10^{n}(\\cos \\left(\\frac{nπ}{2}\\right) - \\textrm{i}\\sin \\left(\\frac{nπ}{2}\\right))] \\\\\n&= 10^{n}(A\\cos \\left(\\frac{nπ}{2}\\right) + B\\sin \\left(\\frac{nπ}{2}\\right))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nA = C+D, \\qquad B = \\textrm i(C-D)\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129046 :question/id #uuid "9f13580e-2a41-52f0-9b69-d881c85683e0" :question/math-academy-id "e-9601" :question/problem "Consider the recurrence relation\n$a_n = -6a_{n-1} - 12a_{n-2}, \\quad a_0 =-1, \\quad a_1=3+\\sqrt{3}$.\nGiven that the solution takes the form\n$a_n = \\big(2\\sqrt{3}\\big)^n \\left(A\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{5n\\pi}{6}\\right) + B\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{5n\\pi}{6}\\right) \\right)$,\nfind the value of $A + B$." :question/worked-solution "The general solution to the recurrence relation is\n\n$$\na_n = \\big(2\\sqrt{3}\\big)^n \\left(A\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{5n\\pi}{6}\\right) + B\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{5n\\pi}{6}\\right) \\right)\n$$\n\nWe find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $a_0 = -1$ into the general solution gives\n$(2\\sqrt{3})^{0}(A\\cos \\left(\\frac{0 \\cdot 5π}{6}\\right) + B\\sin \\left(\\frac{0 \\cdot 5π}{6}\\right))|=-1; 1 \\cdot (A \\cdot 1 + B \\cdot 0)|=-1; A|=-1$.\n- Substituting $a_1=3+\\sqrt{3}$ and $A=-1$ into the general solution gives\n$(2\\sqrt{3})^{1}(-1 \\cdot \\cos \\left(\\frac{1 \\cdot 5π}{6}\\right) + B\\sin \\left(\\frac{1 \\cdot 5π}{6}\\right))|= 3 + \\sqrt{3}; 2\\sqrt{3}(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} + B \\cdot \\frac{1}{2})|= 3 + \\sqrt{3}; 3 + \\sqrt{3}B|= 3 + \\sqrt{3}; B|= 1$.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nA + B = -1 + 1 = 0\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= (2\\sqrt{3})^{n}(\\sin \\left(\\frac{5nπ}{6}\\right) - \\cos \\left(\\frac{5nπ}{6}\\right))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129047 :question/id #uuid "bf1a806e-e676-5cd9-8d42-2995623b2941" :question/math-academy-id "e-9602" :question/problem "Solve the recurrence relation\n$a_n =2a_{n-1} - 2a_{n-2}, \\quad a_0=2, \\quad a_1=5$." :question/worked-solution "Moving all terms of our recurrence relation to the left-hand side, we obtain\n\n$$\na_n - 2a_{n-1} + 2a_{n-2} = 0\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the roots of the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\lambda^2 - 2\\lambda + 2 = 0\n$$\n\nThis equation can be solved by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ^{2} - 2λ + 2 &= 0 \\\\\n(λ^{2} - 2λ + 1) - 1 + 2 &= 0 \\\\\n(λ - 1)^{2} + 1 &= 0 \\\\\n(λ - 1)^{2} &= -1 \\\\\nλ - 1 &= ± \\textrm{i} \\\\\nλ &= 1 ± \\textrm{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1 = 1 + \\textrm{i}, \\qquad \\lambda_2 = 1 - \\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nExpressing our solutions in polar form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ_{1} &= \\sqrt{2}(\\cos \\left(\\frac{π}{4}\\right) + \\textrm{i}\\sin \\left(\\frac{π}{4}\\right)), \\\\\nλ_{2} &= \\sqrt{2}(\\cos \\left(\\frac{π}{4}\\right) - \\textrm{i}\\sin \\left(\\frac{π}{4}\\right))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution to the recurrence relation is\n\n$$\na_n = \\big(\\sqrt{2}\\big)^n \\left(A\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi}{4}\\right) + B\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi}{4}\\right)\\right)\n$$\n\nWe now find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions:\n\n- Substituting $a_0 = 2$ into the general solution gives\n$(\\sqrt{2})^{0}(A\\cos \\left(\\frac{0 \\cdot π}{4}\\right) + B\\sin \\left(\\frac{0 \\cdot π}{4}\\right))|= 2; 1 \\cdot (A \\cdot 1 + B \\cdot 0)|= 2; A|= 2$.\n- Substituting $a_1=5$ and $A=2$ into the general solution gives\n$(\\sqrt{2})^{1}(2 \\cdot \\cos \\left(\\frac{1 \\cdot π}{4}\\right) + B\\sin \\left(\\frac{1 \\cdot π}{4}\\right))|= 5; \\sqrt{2} \\cdot (\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{2} + B \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})|= 5; 2 + B|= 5; B|= 3$.\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\na_n = \\big(\\sqrt{2}\\big)^n \\left(2\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi}{4}\\right) + 3\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi}{4}\\right)\\right)\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129073 :question/id #uuid "066f76be-83f6-52fd-ab3f-00786d18f64e" :question/math-academy-id "e-9866" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx = t-\\sin t, \\quad y = 1-\\cos t, \\quad t\\in \\left[0,\\pi\\right].\n$$\n\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS = \\int 2\\pi y\\,\\textrm d s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm d s = \\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS = 2\\pi \\int_{t_1}^{t_2} y \\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from $t_1=0$ to $t_2=\\pi.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\sqrt{{(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\sqrt{1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the identity $\\sin^2 t +\\cos^2 t= 1,$ we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\sqrt{1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\sqrt{1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\sqrt{2-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\sqrt{2(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129074 :question/id #uuid "b7f404a3-8885-5a33-9cfb-1e12a6c745a8" :question/math-academy-id "e-9867" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx = \\sin t, \\quad y = e^t, \\quad t\\in \\left[0,1\\right].\n$$\n\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS = \\int 2\\pi x\\,\\textrm d s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm d s = \\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS = 2\\pi \\int_{t_1}^{t_2} x \\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from $t_1=0$ to $t_2=1.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})={e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{({e}^{t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡t+{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129075 :question/id #uuid "205f4d6b-affa-530e-979b-a394e7a46bd3" :question/math-academy-id "e-9869" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx = 1+3t, \\quad y = 2t^{3/2}, \\quad t\\in \\left[0,1\\right].\n$$\n\nCalculate the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS = \\int 2\\pi x\\,\\textrm d s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm d s = \\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS = 2\\pi \\int_{t_1}^{t_2} x \\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from $t_1=0$ to $t_2=1.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+3t)=3 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3/2})=3{t}^{1/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(1+3t)\\sqrt{{(3)}^{2}+{(3{t}^{1/2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(1+3t)\\sqrt{9+9t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(1+3t)\\sqrt{9(1+t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(1+3t)\\cdot 3\\sqrt{1+t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(1+3t)\\sqrt{1+t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the last integral using the substitution $u = 1+t.$ Then, $1+3t=3u-2$ and $\\textrm{d}u = \\textrm d t.$ We also need to compute new limits, given below.\n\n| **$t$** | $0$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =6\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(1+3t)\\sqrt{1+t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}(3u-2)\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =6\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}(3{u}^{3/2}-2{u}^{1/2})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =6\\pi \\cdot {[\\frac{6{u}^{5/2}}{5}-\\frac{4{u}^{3/2}}{3}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =6\\pi [(\\frac{6({2}^{5/2})}{5}-\\frac{4({2}^{3/2})}{3})-(\\frac{6}{5}-\\frac{4}{3})] \\\\  & =6\\pi [(\\frac{24\\sqrt{2}}{5}-\\frac{8\\sqrt{2}}{3})-(\\frac{6}{5}-\\frac{4}{3})] \\\\  & =6\\pi [\\frac{32\\sqrt{2}}{15}+\\frac{2}{15}] \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pi }{5}(16\\sqrt{2}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129401 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82a340a7-1cb8-5713-a6c0-73e88d9a4f21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186125907} {:answer/id #uuid "8ec2540e-833a-59ec-b158-e41320a2668e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186125908} {:answer/id #uuid "e03523a9-0302-57bf-a2c8-251c6a1e70fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186125909} {:answer/id #uuid "47320515-e4d1-52ac-8b21-d215511c4422" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186125910} {:answer/id #uuid "62c801a9-62f1-574f-bc1b-80ea2e7a8932" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186125911}] :answer-field/id #uuid "fa3745de-a8d8-5125-8613-9346955c5eb7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186125906}] :question/id #uuid "2af8994b-c9e2-5d59-9c51-e7eeafbf6a0d" :question/math-academy-id "q-100300" :question/problem "What is $18 \\div \\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)^{-2} + (\\frac{1}{101} - 0.01)^{0}$?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129412 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0ba94d7-362e-54af-a4dc-5650b2efcf4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}y\\sqrt{1+{csc}^{2}⁡y}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386102} {:answer/id #uuid "0100602d-f98b-513d-9cb0-94a20d3703e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{tan}⁡y\\sqrt{1+{sec}^{2}⁡y}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386103} {:answer/id #uuid "ea707e80-3f6b-5ca4-a2d4-29097932496c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}y\\sqrt{1+{tan}^{4}⁡y}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386104} {:answer/id #uuid "4278f499-f5e5-5ac4-b8e0-fc854a2288e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{1+{sec}^{2}⁡y}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386105} {:answer/id #uuid "028fc07b-3f60-56d1-8edb-1fb1620551ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}y\\sqrt{1+{sec}^{4}⁡y}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386106}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "028fc07b-3f60-56d1-8edb-1fb1620551ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}y\\sqrt{1+{sec}^{4}⁡y}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386106} :answer-field/id #uuid "66b18467-8af9-5ad0-97da-81cd55579f69" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386101}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5c12e390-7cac-5d15-8cb3-07a2b192a6de" :question/math-academy-id "q-104533" :question/problem "Consider the arc of the curve $x=\\operatorname{tan}⁡y$ that lies between $y=0$ and $y=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x=f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${y}_{1}=0$ to ${y}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡y)={sec}^{2}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}y\\sqrt{1+{({sec}^{2}⁡y)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}y\\sqrt{1+{sec}^{4}⁡y}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129414 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8edd53f6-dbf6-5462-8d89-178b22fe4c2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2π\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\frac{2y - y^{3}}{\\sqrt{1 + 2y - y^{3}}}dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186125985} {:answer/id #uuid "ac863c0c-12ac-5af9-989a-512c1472148c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2π\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2}\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{1 + 2y - y^{3}}}dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186125986} {:answer/id #uuid "eb4ff329-cbec-572f-819b-87f004c11751" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2π\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}y\\sqrt{9y^{4} - 12y^{2} + 5}dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186125987} {:answer/id #uuid "f1ee36fc-7458-5f3c-93ba-3fe804a66343" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2π\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}(2y - y^{3})\\sqrt{9y^{4} - 12y^{2} + 5}dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186125988} {:answer/id #uuid "4ec62c73-022a-53f5-a458-fe05bdd41ede" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2π\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2}y^{2}\\sqrt{9y^{4} - 12y^{2} + 5}dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186125989}] :answer-field/id #uuid "2b744d30-5cec-5215-9c0e-5a7df0803be6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186125984} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b2f7bce1-acec-5122-9b90-9da8ee57060e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}y\\sqrt{9{y}^{4}-12{y}^{2}+5}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386379} {:answer/id #uuid "4ed19fb8-09e0-52e9-8d4a-df6005e5368e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\frac{2y-{y}^{3}}{\\sqrt{1+2y-{y}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386380} {:answer/id #uuid "2ace5a33-02d7-59a6-ae9c-36363a40e9c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{1+2y-{y}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386381} {:answer/id #uuid "7c0bb42a-4c29-56e9-a416-95a58379d2c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}(2y-{y}^{3})\\sqrt{9{y}^{4}-12{y}^{2}+5}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386382} {:answer/id #uuid "97422e77-e71b-5cfe-b628-574a953fb8ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{2}\\sqrt{9{y}^{4}-12{y}^{2}+5}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386383}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2f7bce1-acec-5122-9b90-9da8ee57060e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}y\\sqrt{9{y}^{4}-12{y}^{2}+5}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386379} :answer-field/id #uuid "3a4f0f1b-31e8-5e50-941a-2a0b1055c653" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386378}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "18821903-024e-5a0d-8496-6fcfa9ff5b2a" :question/math-academy-id "q-109179" :question/problem "![](images/dc/dc94ee46e2b9381afb6f62b74c67d76d137f857c3f78f36df2fdad5661b114b3.png)\n\nConsider the arc of the curve $x=2y-{y}^{3}$ that lies in the first quadrant between its $y$-intercepts. What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x=f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we find where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y-{y}^{3} & =0 \\\\ y(2-{y}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ y & =0,\\pm \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=\\sqrt{2}.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(2y-{y}^{3})=2-3{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(2-3{y}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}y\\sqrt{9{y}^{4}-12{y}^{2}+5}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129429 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d0a04ba-2e9e-5db6-acf0-5238f2b90d29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a_{n} = 2^{n/2}(A\\cos \\left(\\frac{nπ}{2}\\right) + B\\sin \\left(\\frac{nπ}{2}\\right))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126075} {:answer/id #uuid "c21b81a1-4507-58a7-b37b-678b920497d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a_{n}