 {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "π\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π/4}\\cos^{2} t\\sin t\\sqrt{16\\cos^{2} t - 1}dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126423} {:answer/id #uuid "0e1efc18-e010-553c-8f9e-6780a7db962b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2π\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π/4}\\cos t\\sin^{2} t\\sqrt{9\\cos^{2} t + 1}dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126424} {:answer/id #uuid "5d3cca44-c196-5525-9e7e-045dc8583ccf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2π\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π/4}\\cos^{2} t\\sin t\\sqrt{8\\cos^{2} t + 1}dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126425} {:answer/id #uuid "6d91462c-2690-56e5-aea0-a32058232638" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "π\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π/4}\\cos^{2} t\\sin t\\sqrt{9\\sin^{2} t - 1}dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126426} {:answer/id #uuid "797c50af-4028-5fe2-b2e9-fe0fbf0b46cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2π\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π/4}\\cos t\\sin t\\sqrt{16\\cos^{2} t + 1}dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126427}] :answer-field/id #uuid "4ef9768c-3daa-5487-b479-dda4d60c023e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126422} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "043bca6d-e51a-51ba-a09b-99b382b8da65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{16{cos}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388581} {:answer/id #uuid "48e228c8-2d28-5a1e-86dc-92351d98ff14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{cos}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{16{cos}^{2}⁡t-1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388582} {:answer/id #uuid "7ed5c02d-d9f9-5469-8a00-cecc8190fbae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{cos}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡t-1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388583} {:answer/id #uuid "714e445f-56cb-528c-bafc-6f1c47b69152" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{cos}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{8{cos}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388584} {:answer/id #uuid "c91c616b-eb42-5423-bb57-c0c7b31e310c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t{sin}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{9{cos}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388585}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "043bca6d-e51a-51ba-a09b-99b382b8da65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{16{cos}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388581} :answer-field/id #uuid "23a64857-ac40-56fe-ba2e-3500895417fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388580}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2fe9bcdf-6522-538a-a529-97fa9381c2b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-138944" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}].\n$$\n\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{4{sin}^{2}⁡2t+{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the identity $\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,$ we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{4{sin}^{2}⁡2t+{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{4(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{16{sin}^{2}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡t(16{cos}^{2}⁡t+1)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot \\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\cdot \\sqrt{16{cos}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot |\\operatorname{sin}⁡t|\\cdot \\sqrt{16{cos}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{16{cos}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129488 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "276f10df-f807-54a9-b01f-7cf1b357ec66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529665} {:answer/id #uuid "8f170c4b-c5f7-5356-8a5a-6fd851ea13ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529925} {:answer/id #uuid "a53f81c5-8ba3-5b6b-9818-2872dc69946f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529990} {:answer/id #uuid "b86bdc8d-5bf9-5ea7-8414-b3dab58e55bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530042} {:answer/id #uuid "e9f6ac1e-5d34-5fa1-a3ce-c20f5afa43d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530163}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a53f81c5-8ba3-5b6b-9818-2872dc69946f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529990} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a8fb9e3-9863-5703-95c1-0d8295fc07d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126428}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "497a1af7-923f-5a2e-82d5-147a5d2a1d6d" :question/math-academy-id "q-138945" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=3t,\\,y=4t,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=1.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t)=3 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t)=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}3t\\sqrt{{(3)}^{2}+{(4)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}3t\\sqrt{25}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}3t\\cdot 5\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =30\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129490 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fd56eb4-4c51-5eff-a136-8a8cbe4d2081" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{32\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531963} {:answer/id #uuid "26b58db8-aa9b-5306-acc7-0ae354e19ac8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{48\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532013} {:answer/id #uuid "d30855cf-2561-551f-9f08-0885a43147ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{63\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532385} {:answer/id #uuid "e76641f1-4b1e-5f93-a7e4-384fd90837d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{24\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532432} {:answer/id #uuid "e78f34b4-d5a7-5009-8033-283511cf7d05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{56\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532433}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "26b58db8-aa9b-5306-acc7-0ae354e19ac8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{48\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532013} :answer-field/id #uuid "fded47ac-9666-5e9a-a118-02ceca041d7e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126440}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "294bf3af-6f1c-5f27-8904-a3e6a8a75f11" :question/math-academy-id "q-138970" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=3t-{t}^{3},\\,y=3{t}^{2},\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nCalculate the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=1.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t-{t}^{3})=3-3{t}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2})=6t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}3{t}^{2}\\sqrt{{(3-3{t}^{2})}^{2}+{(6t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}3{t}^{2}\\sqrt{9-18{t}^{2}+9{t}^{4}+36{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}3{t}^{2}\\sqrt{9{t}^{4}+18{t}^{2}+9}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, factoring the expression $9{t}^{4}+18{t}^{2}+9,$ we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}3{t}^{2}\\sqrt{9{t}^{4}+18{t}^{2}+9}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}3{t}^{2}\\sqrt{9({t}^{4}+2{t}^{2}+1)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}3{t}^{2}\\cdot 3\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+2{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =18\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{({t}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =18\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}\\cdot |{t}^{2}+1|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =18\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}({t}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =18\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{4}+{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =18\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{4}+{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =18\\pi \\cdot {[\\frac{{t}^{5}}{5}+\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =18\\pi [(\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{1}{3})-(\\frac{0}{5}+\\frac{0}{3})] \\\\  & =18\\pi \\cdot \\frac{8}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{48\\pi }{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129491 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cfec902a-5cf6-5bd9-be1a-e3d96e022a93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}π\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π}\\sin^{2} tdt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126447} {:answer/id #uuid "f93032b9-b3fc-5327-adc8-0e6f60a52086" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4π\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π}\\cos^{2} tdt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126448} {:answer/id #uuid "3efaf6b4-0d42-576d-955e-71bfd02a85ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8π\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π}\\cos tdt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126449} {:answer/id #uuid "5a771360-6985-5115-abec-e29fd81217f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8π\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π}\\sin tdt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126450} {:answer/id #uuid "60a911b4-cf87-501e-9618-b1012b51b0c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}π\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π}\\sin t\\cos tdt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126451}] :answer-field/id #uuid "d9753294-5be4-52de-ae49-8ba1ea6b80bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126446} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14487349-5e36-54ef-b555-7e68b1718167" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388587} {:answer/id #uuid "cee0d74a-83cd-5176-8f5a-2e4575be8319" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388588} {:answer/id #uuid "ac546779-6d22-58f5-83b0-983de3177f40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388589} {:answer/id #uuid "9335e776-53f3-549d-88f0-a3477bc64cf7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388590} {:answer/id #uuid "630ba254-d590-5a7a-87c2-7823432453fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388591}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "14487349-5e36-54ef-b555-7e68b1718167" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388587} :answer-field/id #uuid "bbd66017-723a-53a9-aea0-dff9e46c0339" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388586}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "67f3e12f-3cac-5a99-84d0-463e68f2be51" :question/math-academy-id "q-138981" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,t\\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$\n\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=\\pi .$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{4{cos}^{2}⁡t+4{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{4({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot 2\\cdot \\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the identity ${sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t=1,$ we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =8\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129493 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07e6e50c-1780-5336-8367-37225b70e0ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}t{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266214} {:answer/id #uuid "7fd0d5bf-9bb4-5bc8-b32d-39e668f4af0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}t\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+16}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267270} {:answer/id #uuid "9b55cd86-b511-5139-9c4c-98037b72e09f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}t\\sqrt{{e}^{t}+16{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267545} {:answer/id #uuid "a5e6de98-1570-52a5-a67a-55fa300d8e5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{{e}^{t}+4}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267645} {:answer/id #uuid "c782f5d2-3380-5529-b43c-f9cdff435a43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267932}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7fd0d5bf-9bb4-5bc8-b32d-39e668f4af0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}t\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+16}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267270} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc1b288e-afc2-5214-91d4-4d1e966b3f61" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268799}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c7215440-4058-5eea-908f-bc93626bc674" :question/math-academy-id "q-139069" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx={e}^{t},\\,y=4t,\\,t\\in [0,2].\n$$\n\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=2.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})={e}^{t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t)=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}4t\\sqrt{{({e}^{t})}^{2}+{(4)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}t\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+16}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129504 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c2703af-9466-5bfe-b4be-6b83ac22c669" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126525} {:answer/id #uuid "cf58a05e-6426-5bb8-8231-daefdfe1b35a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126526} {:answer/id #uuid "e030ad9f-a5b5-582f-939f-64d484910356" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126527} {:answer/id #uuid "3310ee92-258d-50a0-8117-218100854be9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126528} {:answer/id #uuid "3a3f2756-8915-5175-ae43-ff1f5dcb79fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126529}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf58a05e-6426-5bb8-8231-daefdfe1b35a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126526} :answer-field/id #uuid "ace18637-bd27-5171-b992-40bdb4d2ac91" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126524}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "297a0864-bd1f-5e14-96f0-53d43c9309da" :question/math-academy-id "q-160365" :question/problem "Using the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=y-2x,\\,v=y+x,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the lines\n\n$$\ny=2x-1,\\,y=2x+1,\\,y=1-x,\\,y=2-x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=y-2x,\\,v=y+x\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=y-2x$ into the equations of the first and second lines:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=2x-1 & ⟹\\, & y-2x=-1 & ⟹\\, & u=-1 \\\\  & y=2x+1 & ⟹\\, & y-2x=1 & ⟹\\, & u=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=y+x$ into the equations of the third and fourth lines:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=1-x & ⟹\\, & y+x=1 & ⟹\\, & v=1 \\\\  & y=2-x & ⟹\\, & y+x=2 & ⟹\\, & v=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 1,\\,1\\le v\\le 2\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/21/2116f558e3712a08c13d49238054336dcb60efc7d9602bed220be8115d392be7.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|-\\frac{1}{3}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{∬}_{Δ}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\text{Area}(Δ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129510 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f9a5a2a-545e-5d46-b19c-4068b1213e4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371894} {:answer/id #uuid "63280751-8551-52ab-aad4-1f353dc9cff0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371895} {:answer/id #uuid "ae18d589-8d64-5813-919f-d918e476547f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371896} {:answer/id #uuid "3e4a2890-0cfb-55f6-8c16-0d5316c103ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371897} {:answer/id #uuid "901eef6c-1573-5902-bc29-0f98a541c427" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371898}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "63280751-8551-52ab-aad4-1f353dc9cff0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371895} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc626db0-fa1b-5235-9810-17d15560c467" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371893}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "846ced15-849c-5a63-8d00-4edf7b2a4931" :question/math-academy-id "q-160864" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to calculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the following lines.\n\n$$\n3y=5x+2,\\,3y=5x-2,\\,2x=y,\\,4x=2y+3\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe'll assume this transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv,\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in five steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nNotice that we can rewrite the equations of our curves as follows:\n\n$$\n5x-3y=-2,\\,5x-3y=2,\\,4x-2y=0,\\,4x-2y=3\n$$\n\nThis suggests the following change of variables:\n\n$$\nu=5x-3y,\\,v=4x-2y\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=5x-3y$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 5x-3y=-2\\,⟹\\,u=-2 \\\\  & 5x-3y=2\\,⟹\\,u=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=4x-2y$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 4x-2y=0\\,⟹\\,v=0 \\\\  & 4x-2y=3\\,⟹\\,v=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-2\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 3\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/46/46b808722b24d467ba1015214268658e601779801ca54e8892b92b527ef9ee1d.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}5 & -3 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-10+12 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{1}{2}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{∬}_{Δ}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (2-(-2))\\cdot (3-0) \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129538 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3056ba5d-f7d1-55a3-8c91-5da94f695f90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9-4\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426091} {:answer/id #uuid "32392a3b-31a7-5a44-8746-b4c85858d143" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5-2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426092} {:answer/id #uuid "77d105e7-3ddf-576d-b7fd-6524428a349e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4-2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426108} {:answer/id #uuid "7faf887c-1e04-5044-ade7-d44593ea0768" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8-3\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426111} {:answer/id #uuid "d52f3d90-a693-584a-a56c-1a94156d2638" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10-4\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426136}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d52f3d90-a693-584a-a56c-1a94156d2638" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10-4\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426136} :answer-field/id #uuid "1239f0fe-7d29-5b4f-895a-1d599c74b28e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126725}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "41c034f9-14b4-5bc4-9351-1214f69c2032" :question/math-academy-id "q-162990" :question/problem "![](images/0a/0a85b0be5f8e5db2cf4d1716e4afe4a2780c500d94a1ce282a9d0044f93ba084.png)\n\nConsider the plate that occupies the region $D$ enclosed by the polar circles $r=1$ and $r=\\sqrt{3}$ and the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ in the first quadrant, as shown above. The mass density function of the plate is given by $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{6}{x}.$ Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (r,\\theta ),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFirst, note that the region $D$ in polar coordinates can be expressed as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le \\sqrt{3},\\,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}\\}.\n$$\n\nAlso, the mass density function in polar coordinates is \n\n$$\n\\lambda (r,\\theta )=\\frac{6}{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }=\\frac{6}{r}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{3}}(\\frac{6}{r}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta ){r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{3}}6{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =({\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{3}}6{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =2{r}^{3}{|}_{1}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {|}_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =2(3\\sqrt{3}-1)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}-1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =(3\\sqrt{3}-1)\\cdot (\\sqrt{3}-1) \\\\  & =10-4\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129547 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ac9e4f5-f9bb-5cb3-a441-2df03912c587" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{3}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278695} {:answer/id #uuid "38f1a566-7c97-5337-9d4b-783c036260ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{44}{15}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279090} {:answer/id #uuid "7164fd3c-39d3-5e10-b029-8c4e41a2418d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{5}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279620} {:answer/id #uuid "7d0eb46f-ac14-5ea1-8812-9357a23f4fe2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{5}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279708} {:answer/id #uuid "7fb3e82d-3027-5337-9ee7-e79c84a52cda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{49}{15}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279739}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "38f1a566-7c97-5337-9d4b-783c036260ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{44}{15}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279090} :answer-field/id #uuid "16e9d6ee-24d0-514b-86ab-3da50cc273c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280872}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f66a0c3f-e290-5a0d-824c-35593ff13ee1" :question/math-academy-id "q-163172" :question/problem "A rectangular plate of mass $\\frac{4}{5}\\,\\text{kg}$ and uniform mass density occupies the region\n$D={(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,-2\\le y\\le 2},$\nwhere $x$ and $y$ are measured in meters. Find the moment of inertia of the plate about a line perpendicular to $D$ at a distance of $\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{m}$ from a parallel line passing through its center of mass." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina of mass $M$ and a line ${l}_{M}$ perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass, the parallel axis theorem states that the moment of inertia of the lamina about a line $l$ parallel to ${l}_{M}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{l}={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M,\n$$\n\nwhere $d$ is the distance between the lines, and ${I}_{M}$ and ${I}_{l}$ are the moments of inertia about the lines ${l}_{M}$ and $l,$ respectively.\n\nThe area of the rectangular plate $D$ is $A=2\\cdot 4=8\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}.$\nSince the plate has a uniform density, the mass density function is given by\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{M}{A}=\\frac{\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)}{8}=\\frac{1}{10}\\,\\text{kg}/{\\text{m}}^{2},\n$$\n\nand its center of mass is its centroid:\n\n$$\n(\\overset{¯}{x},\\overset{¯}{y})=(\\frac{-1+1}{2},\\frac{-2+2}{2})=(0,0)\n$$\n\nSince the center of mass is at the origin, the moment of inertia about the perpendicular line through the center of mass is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{M} & ={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\frac{1}{10}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{[{x}^{2}y+\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}]}_{y=-2}^{y=2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}4{x}^{2}+\\frac{16}{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}{[\\frac{4}{3}{x}^{3}+\\frac{16}{3}x]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}(\\frac{8}{3}+\\frac{32}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\left(\\frac{40}{3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the parallel axis theorem, the moment of inertia about a line $\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{m}$ from the center of mass is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}+(\\sqrt{2})^{2}\\cdot \\frac{4}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{8}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{44}{15}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129559 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e544e3a-d529-59a4-9b74-e3201728f32a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{\\sqrt{531}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288692} {:answer/id #uuid "7ee7d482-5263-53cf-badc-66a9de382349" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{\\sqrt{31}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288893} {:answer/id #uuid "9a597216-5223-5ff3-9187-337e34abe4ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{\\sqrt{21}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289065} {:answer/id #uuid "b7c3a230-584c-5e9d-bdb7-4261139aa3fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{\\sqrt{21}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289250} {:answer/id #uuid "bf51de70-a0eb-546b-824e-fc252f8b1f7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{\\sqrt{31}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289292}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b7c3a230-584c-5e9d-bdb7-4261139aa3fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{\\sqrt{21}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289250} :answer-field/id #uuid "e636597e-dc6b-528d-b889-144be088df47" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290216}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e4be94e7-eb10-5b1f-8a9d-fe5e79ca236b" :question/math-academy-id "q-23273" :question/problem "The point $P$ is located in the third quadrant and lies on the unit circle. The $x$-coordinate of $P$ is $-\\frac{2}{5}.$ What is the $y$-coordinate of $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the point $P$ lies on the unit circle, it must satisfy the Pythagorean theorem: \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-\\frac{2}{5}$ into the above and solving for $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {\\left(-\\frac{2}{5}\\right)}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\left(\\frac{4}{25}\\right)+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =1-\\frac{4}{25} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =\\frac{21}{25} \\\\ y & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{21}{25}} \\\\ y & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{21}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we select the negative answer since $y$ is negative in the third quadrant. So our final answer is $y=-\\frac{\\sqrt{21}}{5}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129564 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "165dd9be-8787-52e5-af21-883b13bcaa26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288182} {:answer/id #uuid "4d423f60-e247-5193-81e2-deec61035167" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "cos⁡\\theta =-\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288568} {:answer/id #uuid "6dc87fcb-e311-53c6-a0b4-26ad31d76151" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "cos⁡\\theta =-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288800} {:answer/id #uuid "dbe9fb75-9a26-5f50-935f-5dab8b9ff80a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289495} {:answer/id #uuid "e7eaf9e2-a3f6-5edd-a709-9deef4b12842" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "cos⁡\\theta =-\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289574}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4d423f60-e247-5193-81e2-deec61035167" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "cos⁡\\theta =-\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288568} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1a5a4c7-f9f3-5318-9f7c-d8b90cf164b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290233}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "83599989-7e1a-5f2f-adf9-0de9e207ef83" :question/math-academy-id "q-24247" :question/problem "![](images/c2/c2644fc906d4588bc39aa92d887836ff8942f5eb4b28de1baf3dc1030d2dca67.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{3},$ what is the value of $cos⁡\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/49/4942cec8983dd2bddddd0ba2f8f9332b1b82f3999a44adb138b905457d39c633.png)\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $cos⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{3}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{1}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{8}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the negative answer since $cos⁡\\theta $ is negative in the second quadrant. So, $cos⁡\\theta =-\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129574 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cd842061-0520-57ab-9052-b9bd73575d86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = \\frac{nπ}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126930} {:answer/id #uuid "dbedaef7-78c9-5317-9342-5cf9c36bb098" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = nπ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126931} {:answer/id #uuid "30068548-9d23-5f14-b66b-a6bc39640ed3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = \\frac{nπ}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126932} {:answer/id #uuid "44e3eb56-563f-5ff8-841d-c688ef4fc513" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 2nπ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126933} {:answer/id #uuid "758606fd-e9eb-51e6-9339-f8efa4769461" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = \\frac{π}{4} + \\frac{nπ}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126934}] :answer-field/id #uuid "6c2a62b1-0147-589d-9f4f-dda406e53e91" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126929}] :question/id #uuid "8717d3bc-12ae-5d26-96d5-b240347d3292" :question/math-academy-id "q-29276" :question/problem "Which of the following expressions gives all of the vertical asymptotes of the function $f(x) = \\cot \\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right) + 1$? Assume that $n$ is an integer."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129611 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "459c1e1e-300d-5a95-95e1-1797315a2764" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321699} {:answer/id #uuid "691c0401-eba2-540b-8580-27fee56adb62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321971} {:answer/id #uuid "e1f5b50c-9aa3-5dc7-aba2-56bbb5f57ef9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322913} {:answer/id #uuid "ed1b6b23-7ee8-5f5f-92da-74f2a4424f79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323018} {:answer/id #uuid "ff5bca53-6095-5a85-9dae-a896b459a08b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323161}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ed1b6b23-7ee8-5f5f-92da-74f2a4424f79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323018} :answer-field/id #uuid "9104a251-cd2f-519d-a2ba-8be75fd21207" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323451}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6743007e-663f-5413-8cfe-af47a6a07879" :question/math-academy-id "q-37602" :question/problem "Consider the surface $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{3}+yz)=3.$ Given that $n=⟨a,b,c⟩$ is a non-zero normal vector to the surface at the point $(e,{e}^{2},-e),$ what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define  $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{3}+yz).$ Then, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=3.$  \n\nNow, we find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at $(e,{e}^{2},-e):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{3}+yz))i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{3}+yz))j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{3}+yz))k \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2{x}^{3}+yz}\\cdot 6{x}^{2})i+(\\frac{1}{2{x}^{3}+yz}\\cdot z)j+(\\frac{1}{2{x}^{3}+yz}\\cdot y)k \\\\  & =\\frac{6{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{3}+yz}\\text{ }i+\\frac{z}{2{x}^{3}+yz}\\text{ }j+\\frac{y}{2{x}^{3}+yz}\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(e,{e}^{2},-e) & =\\frac{6{e}^{2}}{2{e}^{3}+{e}^{2}(-e)}\\text{ }i+\\frac{(-e)}{2{e}^{3}+{e}^{2}(-e)}\\text{ }j+\\frac{{e}^{2}}{2{e}^{3}+{e}^{2}(-e)}\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{e}\\text{ }i-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}\\text{ }j+\\frac{1}{e}\\text{ }k \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{6}{e},\\,-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}},\\,\\frac{1}{e}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our normal vector is $n=⟨a,b,c⟩=⟨\\frac{6}{e},\\,-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}},\\,\\frac{1}{e}⟩.$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{\\left(\\frac{6}{e}\\right)}{\\left(-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}\\right)}=-6e.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129635 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "452bb7f1-4a41-5c58-88ee-b287c87a5fd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324530} {:answer/id #uuid "52b49f1e-899e-529f-8c6c-961d65cbff93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324648} {:answer/id #uuid "565ad306-f658-5262-966f-808a5c01db3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324691} {:answer/id #uuid "c733909b-26d2-5dcf-baec-398a64834a82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325694} {:answer/id #uuid "de8db398-fab7-5e23-8116-a48003cc8f77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325914}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de8db398-fab7-5e23-8116-a48003cc8f77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325914} :answer-field/id #uuid "962bba9c-ed93-540e-8fff-cd395912615c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326487}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "948a84fa-2589-57e2-8b27-de170c3c28f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-38248" :question/problem "A metal plate occupies the region $D={(x,y):-1\\le y\\le 1,\\,0\\le x\\le {y}^{2}}.$ The charge density of the plate is given by $\\rho (x,y)=x{y}^{2}.$ Find the total charge $Q$ of the plate." :question/worked-solution "The total charge $Q$ of a plate that occupies the region $D$ with charge density $\\rho (x,y)=x{y}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nQ={∬}_{D}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nLet's sketch our region.\n\n![](images/1c/1cbe96aa4bf517302a2329db9f8eb2e9c56873e4d5a18cd5f1cd676543e97086.png)\n\nNotice that $D$ is a type II region. So, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{y}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}x\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{y}^{2}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{{y}^{2}}\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{y}^{2}(\\frac{{y}^{4}}{2}-0)\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{y}^{6}\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{y}^{6}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\frac{1}{2}{[\\frac{{y}^{7}}{7}]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{{1}^{7}}{7}-\\left(-\\frac{{1}^{7}}{7}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total charge of the plate is $Q=\\frac{1}{7}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129728 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "408e29c9-6522-5467-9ab8-def9fe8cfb65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{w}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324490} {:answer/id #uuid "69224e59-e1c5-54cd-8748-b2d597b5cd76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{w}{u}^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324848} {:answer/id #uuid "7b1e869c-a3b7-584b-8e0f-e99423947eb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{w}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325004} {:answer/id #uuid "e98f16dc-8225-5762-aa80-dca5e80b6645" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2{u}^{2}\\sqrt{w}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326027} {:answer/id #uuid "fb5ec600-18ee-54e3-bb28-7c22c6783223" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2{u}^{2}\\sqrt{w}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326182}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e98f16dc-8225-5762-aa80-dca5e80b6645" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2{u}^{2}\\sqrt{w}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326027} :answer-field/id #uuid "65802821-8118-5ece-8435-a69816ad88f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326394}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f85af4c0-4520-5614-952b-cb9d6d06c6c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-39300" :question/problem "Consider the transformation $T:(u,v,w)\\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)).$ The inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}(x,y,z)\\to (u(x,y,z),v(x,y,z),w(x,y,z))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\sqrt{y}+z \\\\ {y}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T.$\n\n*Hint: You can assume that $x\\ne 0$ and $y>0.$*" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nu(x,y,z)=\\frac{1}{x},\\,y(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{y}+z,\\,z(x,y,z)={y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}} & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂z} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =2y & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂z} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}} & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2y & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}} & 1 \\\\ 2y & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting down $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}$ in terms of $u,$ $v,$ and $w,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}} & =\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)^{2}\\cdot 2\\sqrt{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & ={u}^{2}\\cdot 2\\sqrt{w} \\\\  & =2{u}^{2}\\sqrt{w}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & ={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2{u}^{2}\\sqrt{w}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129735 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bb3570cc-07c3-575c-bd5a-d004022fdc86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(- ∞,-7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127892} {:answer/id #uuid "c9a1b692-c894-51c4-b710-1a65703409e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-7, ∞)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127893} {:answer/id #uuid "007df076-76b7-5b84-bdd2-e54c00a02e45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(7, ∞)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127894} {:answer/id #uuid "0e9cc3f3-da8d-5178-90e1-ed65b4edd8a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[- 7, ∞)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127895} {:answer/id #uuid "1f565e20-8ea0-5beb-b5fc-f15124f861ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(- ∞, 7]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127896}] :answer-field/id #uuid "4ce7a742-2c7b-5ae3-b9e1-c7c95f8a4650" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127891}] :question/id #uuid "db48c317-7a15-5413-8128-a00d1b41c102" :question/math-academy-id "q-39423" :question/problem "Find the largest interval of continuity for the function $f(x) = \\log_{5} \\left(\\frac{x + 7}{x^{2} + 4}\\right)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129751 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a3dfa61-4983-5101-a590-b7cae3069606" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324062} {:answer/id #uuid "5975b6e2-4d63-5522-bc15-85311b4304c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324714} {:answer/id #uuid "a5d3c1d8-1ca4-5893-ae40-8a4995898733" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325383} {:answer/id #uuid "c5fd3b47-8f20-518a-a996-2dbd4fa2edc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{40}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325679} {:answer/id #uuid "fcb623d2-5638-5465-9671-82a386df23f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326195}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5fd3b47-8f20-518a-a996-2dbd4fa2edc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{40}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325679} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d44cd8a-7ab2-5c59-a740-4c19610ad44c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326377}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9ad6bc33-7c9a-5d03-8204-18b81f486eea" :question/math-academy-id "q-39669" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T:(u,v,w)\\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)),$ given that \n\n$$\nu=2x+y,\\,v=-x+3y-2z,\\,w=3y+4z.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =2 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =3 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂z} & =-2 \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =3 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂z} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix}-1\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2(12+6)-1(-4-0) \\\\  & =40.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & ={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{40}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129755 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "433bf40e-b58f-5f73-acd3-043724f4f0a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (-1,1)\\cup (1,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324514} {:answer/id #uuid "b5d08469-ad7b-5338-83f8-aa4eda61a77a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325534} {:answer/id #uuid "ba4f7279-6b09-5d5a-b7dd-914bf38083f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (-1,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325571} {:answer/id #uuid "eab55c63-7bc8-5884-94c9-5575e81cf09c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,1)\\cup (1,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326040} {:answer/id #uuid "f0a36cbf-0b55-59cd-a76e-8d0bcaae5e9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,1)\\cup (1,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326095}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "433bf40e-b58f-5f73-acd3-043724f4f0a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (-1,1)\\cup (1,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324514} :answer-field/id #uuid "c279fba3-e32a-5884-932a-62cb2020e6bd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326573}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bdf79360-c3a2-5f70-b20e-0e9fca989978" :question/math-academy-id "q-39765" :question/problem "Find the largest union of intervals on which the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}-1}\\right)$ is continuous." :question/worked-solution "The function is a composition of two functions, a rational function, and a sine function.\n\nThe sine is continuous at all points in its domain, and its domain consists of all real numbers. So, we focus on the rational function.\n\nLikewise, the rational function $\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}-1}$ is continuous at all points in its domain. To find its domain, we start by factoring the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{(x-1)(x+1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis function is continuous everywhere except at the excluded points $x=-1$ and $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, the domain our original function consists of the union of the intervals $(-\\infty ,-1),$ $(-1,1),$ and $(1,\\infty ),$ namely\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (-1,1)\\cup (1,\\infty )\n$$\n\nand the function is also continuous on these intervals."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129775 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c61dc7c0-d3f8-5a52-a767-d5a5950ed61b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\tan θ =-4\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128132} {:answer/id #uuid "3c3acfbd-450b-529e-9e81-f6898d0afc15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\tan θ =-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128133} {:answer/id #uuid "425120b8-9a7a-509b-b2b1-16f549aee0d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\tan θ = \\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128134} {:answer/id #uuid "51495d9a-03f0-5f49-aeab-3c6ea5811fbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\tan θ =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128135} {:answer/id #uuid "681b517b-c0da-555d-9464-011b10f62e6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\tan θ = \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128136}] :answer-field/id #uuid "a1f2f1dc-9d19-53e5-af38-cc9ea5f6ad94" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128131} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d2ab46d8-4b20-5cb0-bd03-d4843745957e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "tan⁡\\theta =-4\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402771} {:answer/id #uuid "1281daa1-21d2-521b-ba7b-2f63be883923" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "tan⁡\\theta =\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402772} {:answer/id #uuid "cf49d4c9-aad2-5d7f-bca3-cd2582a8d854" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "tan⁡\\theta =\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402773} {:answer/id #uuid "1efc176a-2f08-5690-b21d-dbaa2aa9e6f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "tan⁡\\theta =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402774} {:answer/id #uuid "9257c9b4-8a20-5a75-b76e-4b9c11a339a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "tan⁡\\theta =-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402775}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1efc176a-2f08-5690-b21d-dbaa2aa9e6f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "tan⁡\\theta =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402774} :answer-field/id #uuid "737f5b1b-3e66-5582-9362-63911dfedfe9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402770}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "299c91a4-6289-5d24-ace1-495b52634a62" :question/math-academy-id "q-42043" :question/problem "![](images/91/919b9e8f2a9493fd0dda3e0d7bf66de9af054fc4ed9c0fcf7a68329fdbbaffdd.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2},$ what is the value of $tan⁡\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/94/948610f67267e6ec1b845d2b4e1bc4030b81a1080bcb9a44d9f974ff78c3f7b0.png)\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $cos⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{1}{4}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{4}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the negative answer since $cos⁡\\theta $ is negative in the second quadrant. So, $cos⁡\\theta =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $tan⁡\\theta =\\frac{sin⁡\\theta }{cos⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\ntan⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}{\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)}=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129782 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1db175ad-694b-5bb9-959a-3d6e0b2057a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327192} {:answer/id #uuid "287568e1-57a3-5493-8522-6c1936081561" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327277} {:answer/id #uuid "6b8dcb34-da39-5684-9e5c-886c63a6cb4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327859} {:answer/id #uuid "6bcad715-2c41-5c6c-a271-14ddd1312559" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327863} {:answer/id #uuid "94944c92-3dbf-5f11-9056-1700eb82c373" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328233}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1db175ad-694b-5bb9-959a-3d6e0b2057a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327192} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2a75f1b-46ee-5473-b6c7-6d7a730ef92a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329577}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4126b1b4-8e8d-506d-b0ce-d7e1e1256bf6" :question/math-academy-id "q-43404" :question/problem "Calculate the period of the function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "Given a function in the form $f(x)=A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(Bx+C)+D,$ the period $T$ is given by $\\frac{2\\pi }{B}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=\\frac{5}{3}.$ Therefore, the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{\\left(\\frac{5}{3}\\right)}=\\frac{6\\pi }{5}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129783 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b2d63b85-b373-511e-a9fb-f777ea605d5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "70\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{t} + 212" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128180} {:answer/id #uuid "c57cfff8-c59c-5f28-ae0d-25b20516e12f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "212\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{t} + 70" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128181} {:answer/id #uuid "d371b9dd-050a-5254-b291-e562848a6e29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "212(2)^{t} + 70" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128182} {:answer/id #uuid "eb2bed11-4f42-53fe-b43b-df1c5fcdc5ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "142\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{t} + 70" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128183} {:answer/id #uuid "6611374d-89f4-577a-9129-def5ffcfe904" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "142(2)^{t} + 70" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128184}] :answer-field/id #uuid "06d687f8-aeb1-5fda-af4b-f515e278f483" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128179}] :question/id #uuid "a0d53cdf-1b74-5904-b2dc-755b9080d66e